# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 2: Person Description
#
# The WHO task for the winter street photo of sub-lesson 1: who is present, and
# 1–2 of them described visually. The examiner's second prompt on the picture
# task is almost always "pick one person and describe them" — clothes, what they
# are doing, roughly how old they look — so this drills exactly that.
#
# The photo detail this describes: a woman in the foreground on the left in a
# white jacket with a long red scarf, carrying a dark blue bag; a small child in
# the middle further back in a pink coat and a red hat, running with its arms up;
# a man on the left who looks young, walking behind the child. Several people
# further back carry rucksacks.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 — no @question prompts, no first-person opinion.
# A depicted person's visible state (`D'Kand ass glécklech`, `Hie gesäit jonk
# aus`) is observation and is wanted: the child is mid-run with its arms up and
# the man's face is visible, so a stranger can check both against the photo.
#
# `Ech mengen et leeft mat sengem Papp` from the source description ships as a
# HEDGED sentence rather than a flat claim — the family relation is genuinely an
# inference, and the hedge is what keeps it checkable. That is the same licensed
# non-observation the theme's 03 is built on.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as the rest of the theme, with the guard that appearance
# descriptions make easy to break: attributive adjectives are used ONLY on
# feminine nouns (`eng rout Mutz`, `eng waarm Jackett`), where Luxembourgish
# leaves the adjective uninflected. Masculine and neuter attributives need
# declension the learner has not met, so those are stated predicatively instead
# (`D'Kand … ass glécklech.`, `De Schal ass rout.`) — which is also why there is
# no `e glécklecht Kand` here.
#
# Words sub-lesson 1 already teaches (d'Leit, d'Bild, gesinn, lénks, hannen, …)
# are deliberately NOT repeated — the theme's pass-gate makes 01 a prerequisite
# for 02, and stat keys are shared across the app. Overlap with the Schueberfouer
# theme (d'Fra, trëppelen, jonk, mengen) is accepted: themes are independent and
# all open, so a learner may reach this one first and the vocabulary has to be
# taught somewhere in the path they actually play.
#
# Every blank tile below is a word this theme teaches. The declined `lénkser`
# stays in a fixed frame precisely because it is declined — the learner reads it
# rather than deriving it.
#
# The @fill blocks take the person-description frames from the library in
# .claude/memory/picture-description-theme.md that the other picture theme left
# unused: the "I am describing the person on the … side" opener, the two-garment
# `hunn … un` frame, and the split `gesäit … aus` state frame.

@lesson P2.02 "Groussgaass: Person Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# the photo during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it
# describes the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/groussgaass/img/groussgaass.webp"
@image-alt "The same winter street photo, seen for the people in it: a woman in the foreground in a white jacket and a long red scarf carrying a dark blue bag, a small child in a pink coat and red hat running in the middle of the picture, a young-looking man walking on the left."

# --- People ---
@word d'Kand = the child
@word d'Fra = the woman
@word de Mann = the man
@word de Papp = the father

# --- Clothes ---
@word d'Mutz = the woolly hat
@word d'Jackett = the jacket
@word de Schal = the scarf
@word d'Kleeder = the clothes
@word d'Täsch = the bag
@word de Rucksak = the rucksack
@word unhunn = to have on

# --- Colours ---
@word rout = red
@word blo = blue
@word wäiss = white

# --- What they are doing ---
@word trëppelen = to walk
@word lafen = to run
@word ausgesinn = to look
@word mengen = to think

# --- Describing them ---
@word glécklech = happy
@word jonk = young
@word waarm = warm
@word déck = thick
@word an der Mëtt = in the middle
@word am Vierdergrond = in the foreground

@sentence
@lu D'Kand an der Mëtt vum Bild ass glécklech.
@en The child in the middle of the picture is happy.
@distractor-lu traureg
@distractor-lu Mann
@distractor-en sad
@distractor-en man

@sentence
@lu D'Kand huet eng rout Mutz un.
@en The child has a red hat on.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu Jackett
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en jacket

@sentence
@lu D'Kand leeft an der Foussgängerzon.
@en The child is running in the pedestrian zone.
@distractor-lu trëppelt
@distractor-lu Fra
@distractor-en walking
@distractor-en woman

@sentence
@lu Ech mengen, de Mann ass de Papp vum Kand.
@en I think the man is the child's father.
@distractor-lu Fra
@distractor-lu weess
@distractor-en woman
@distractor-en know

@sentence
@lu De Mann ass lénks an hie gesäit jonk aus.
@en The man is on the left and he looks young.
@distractor-lu riets
@distractor-lu al
@distractor-en right
@distractor-en old

@sentence
@lu Am Vierdergrond trëppelt eng Fra.
@en In the foreground a woman is walking.
@distractor-lu leeft
@distractor-lu Kand
@distractor-en running
@distractor-en child

@sentence
@lu D'Jackett vun der Fra ass wäiss an de Schal ass rout.
@en The woman's jacket is white and the scarf is red.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu Mutz
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en hat

@sentence
@lu D'Fra huet eng blo Täsch.
@en The woman has a blue bag.
@distractor-lu rout
@distractor-lu Rucksak
@distractor-en red
@distractor-en rucksack

@sentence
@lu Hannen huet e Mann e Rucksak un.
@en At the back a man has a rucksack on.
@distractor-lu Täsch
@distractor-lu vir
@distractor-en bag
@distractor-en front

@sentence
@lu D'Kleeder vun de Leit sinn déck a waarm.
@en The people's clothes are thick and warm.
@distractor-lu dënn
@distractor-lu Mutz
@distractor-en thin
@distractor-en hat

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The opener for the "describe one person" prompt. It names WHICH person before
# describing them, which is what stops a candidate drifting between subjects
# mid-answer. ONE blank: `lénkser` is declined and therefore belongs in the fixed
# frame (R4) — blanking it would ask the learner to produce a form nothing
# teaches. `d'` outside the blank leaves only `d'`-taking nouns, and both
# distractors are wrong about who stands in the left foreground.
@fill
@lu Ech beschreiwen d'[Fra] am Vierdergrond op der lénkser Säit.
@en I am describing the [woman] in the foreground on the left side.
@distractor-lu Kand
@distractor-lu Mann
@distractor-en child
@distractor-en man

# The two-garment `hunn … un` frame — the separable `un` sits at the end, an
# order the learner reads rather than builds. The two blanks have different
# determiners fixed in the frame (`eng` feminine, `e` masculine), so no tile fits
# both slots: that is R4 doing the disambiguation and R6 satisfied by word class
# rather than by order. Both distractors are things this woman is not wearing, so
# each is wrong in either blank on the photo as well as on gender.
@fill
@lu Si huet eng waarm [Jackett] an e roude [Schal] un.
@en She has a warm [jacket] and a red [scarf] on.
@distractor-lu Mutz
@distractor-lu Rucksak
@distractor-en hat
@distractor-en rucksack

# The visible-state frame with the split `gesäit … aus`. The verb bracket is the
# pattern being taught, so it stays in the frame and the adjective is the blank.
# Both distractors are states the photo contradicts — the child is mid-stride
# with its arms up, which no stranger would read as tired or sad.
@fill
@lu D'Kand gesäit [glécklech] aus.
@en The child looks [happy].
@distractor-lu midd
@distractor-lu traureg
@distractor-en tired
@distractor-en sad
